        \"content\": {
          \"content_type\": \"text\",
          \"parts\": [
            \"Exactly. If you treat internal properties—like spin, vibration modes, or intrinsic angular momentum—as oscillatory or periodic behaviors, you can encode them as modulations or transformations along the particle’s worldline. Essentially, the worldline isn’t just a geometric trace through spacetime; it can be augmented with additional structure representing internal dynamics.  \
\
In other words, the trajectory becomes a multi-layered object: one layer for position in spacetime, and additional layers for internal states, each of which can be mathematically projected and analyzed along that line. This is how things like spinor representations or phase-space trajectories in classical mechanics capture both “where” the particle is and aspects of “what” it is internally.\"
